WEBVTT

00:02.932 --> 00:07.594
[SPEAKER_00]: Jude in Flock Time, just coming to Retro Island.

00:28.145 --> 00:28.385
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey.

00:32.336 --> 00:33.156
[SPEAKER_01]: Hello, hello, hello.

00:33.436 --> 00:34.377
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey, you ready on?

00:34.997 --> 00:35.897
[SPEAKER_01]: Yep, can you hear me?

00:36.117 --> 00:36.657
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I can.

00:37.277 --> 00:38.018
[SPEAKER_01]: Alright, awesome.

00:38.258 --> 00:39.018
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, great.

00:39.978 --> 00:51.261
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so I stand in line and in the background, if you hear seagulls, it's because I've got my phone set to D&D music on YouTube.

00:51.881 --> 00:53.562
[SPEAKER_01]: So if you hear seagulls, you're not going crazy.

00:53.882 --> 00:54.202
[SPEAKER_00]: Awesome.

01:00.212 --> 01:04.235
[SPEAKER_01]: Alright, I'll stand in line and wait patiently, of course, to talk inside.

01:04.915 --> 01:07.637
[SPEAKER_00]: Alright, so what happens is, this is kind of evening.

01:07.917 --> 01:09.138
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, it's nighttime.

01:10.278 --> 01:18.063
[SPEAKER_00]: And, um... Basically, there may be two or three other people that wanted to leave the ship.

01:18.944 --> 01:24.107
[SPEAKER_00]: And, uh, you know, the captain comes up to you and says, uh, what are your plans, sir?

01:26.093 --> 01:27.133
[SPEAKER_01]: I haven't made any yet.

01:27.314 --> 01:31.337
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you meeting with the other merchants as a group to make a decision?

01:32.558 --> 01:33.739
[SPEAKER_00]: We are, actually.

01:34.760 --> 01:35.601
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you doing that now?

01:37.723 --> 01:39.624
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we'll have a decision by tomorrow.

01:40.886 --> 01:48.352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I wasn't going to go ashore, but I was going to talk to the good sergeant about if diplomatic...

01:51.919 --> 01:56.421
[SPEAKER_01]: Are there any more adventurous merchants among your crew?

02:17.055 --> 02:19.716
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we're speaking to each other about this.

02:19.736 --> 02:26.440
[SPEAKER_01]: In truth, it seems that the Earl of Red Shore is trying to make a marketplace here, even though this portage is much smaller than that in Monmurg.

02:27.020 --> 02:29.141
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't see how it will work.

02:49.214 --> 02:55.638
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the truth is, as many of the ships that we see here, we'd want to have trade with.

02:58.000 --> 03:00.701
[SPEAKER_00]: So if there was a place to trade, we would do so.

03:01.342 --> 03:11.328
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a feeling that to salvage what we can from this journey, we'll be sending out invoices to the ships in this area and seeing what deals can be struck

03:13.367 --> 03:13.887
[SPEAKER_00]: That way.

03:15.588 --> 03:16.509
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have a minute?

03:16.829 --> 03:21.892
[SPEAKER_01]: I can forego talking to the sergeant for perhaps 10 or 15 minutes.

03:21.912 --> 03:23.554
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you come back to my cabin with me?

03:24.774 --> 03:25.435
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly, sir.

03:25.935 --> 03:27.056
[SPEAKER_01]: Perhaps for a glass of wine.

03:27.716 --> 03:28.157
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly.

03:29.898 --> 03:32.840
[SPEAKER_01]: So I come back to the cabin with him.

03:33.380 --> 03:34.601
[SPEAKER_01]: I pour him a glass of wine.

03:37.859 --> 03:39.100
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me speak plainly.

03:40.301 --> 03:49.708
[SPEAKER_01]: As an envoy from Idee, and as you know coming from Scamp, the situation of the war in Idee this past year, that's where I've been.

03:50.869 --> 04:01.098
[SPEAKER_01]: We're in dire need of assistance, and it had been my hope to travel to Monmurg, since they are wealthy and seem...

04:01.991 --> 04:14.059
[SPEAKER_01]: To understand many things about defense of a city and rebuilding, which certainly Marie is in desperate need of, and to entreat with them for assistance or aid.

04:15.180 --> 04:26.728
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any chance that any of the merchants would wish to perhaps not honor the blockade of the Duke of Branson?

04:30.496 --> 04:32.336
[SPEAKER_00]: You mean try and run the blockade?

04:33.317 --> 04:34.157
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, of course.

04:38.018 --> 04:39.558
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a dangerous undertaking.

04:41.098 --> 04:41.719
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

04:42.159 --> 04:42.659
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

04:43.979 --> 04:50.861
[SPEAKER_00]: So you say you are a ambassador, as it were, traveling to Monmurg?

04:51.981 --> 04:53.441
[SPEAKER_01]: I do, yes.

04:53.501 --> 04:55.562
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been fighting in the D against the South.

05:04.498 --> 05:13.061
[SPEAKER_00]: So my question to you, sir, is why is not the Monmurgian Navy escorting the ships that ply these trade lines?

05:14.962 --> 05:22.724
[SPEAKER_00]: They usually are patrolling the oceans all the way up to Gradsul.

05:22.804 --> 05:26.325
[SPEAKER_01]: As I told you, I've been in the deep for the past year.

05:26.665 --> 05:28.446
[SPEAKER_01]: This is my first time returning.

05:28.937 --> 05:32.238
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not an envoy from, or a diplomat from Monmurg.

05:32.538 --> 05:36.380
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm traveling from the D, because I have dealing time.

05:37.360 --> 05:41.542
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, this comes as much of a shock to me as to you.

05:41.682 --> 05:42.102
[SPEAKER_00]: I see.

05:42.402 --> 05:45.563
[SPEAKER_00]: So do you have some influence with Prince Jeon?

05:50.645 --> 05:52.606
[SPEAKER_01]: That is possible.

05:53.286 --> 05:56.227
[SPEAKER_01]: A somewhat temperamental influence, yes.

05:58.459 --> 06:02.723
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in truth, I'll let this be known to you.

06:05.506 --> 06:17.457
[SPEAKER_00]: I think all the merchants here are due to lose quite a bit of money if we are to either trade here in Redshore, Gradsul, or return without trading our wares to Onwal.

06:17.817 --> 06:23.582
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think most of them would want to run the blockade if they could.

06:24.786 --> 06:32.792
[SPEAKER_00]: However, a better solution would be the Monmurgian Navy actually resuming patrols.

06:33.753 --> 06:34.494
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, of course.

06:34.994 --> 06:43.181
[SPEAKER_01]: We can't either retreat Monmurg or the Sea Princes to come back into the waters.

06:43.861 --> 06:46.423
[SPEAKER_01]: We haven't even been there yet.

06:46.523 --> 06:48.084
[SPEAKER_01]: It's been a year, I guess.

06:48.685 --> 06:51.287
[SPEAKER_01]: More to the point, Captain.

06:58.097 --> 07:08.261
[SPEAKER_01]: I do not want to put something this severe on you without your and your police willingness.

07:13.483 --> 07:15.584
[SPEAKER_01]: Should you decide, go to the merchants.

07:15.604 --> 07:17.585
[SPEAKER_01]: I will leave you to speak to the merchants.

07:18.642 --> 07:20.143
[SPEAKER_00]: I will speak among the merchants.

07:20.223 --> 07:21.163
[SPEAKER_00]: I hear what you're saying.

07:21.923 --> 07:26.165
[SPEAKER_00]: And unfortunately, I will be a little...

07:26.245 --> 07:30.087
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm willing to be a little conspiratorial with you, sir.

07:32.568 --> 07:44.293
[SPEAKER_00]: I can tell you, without a doubt, that among the men here, all of us would rather be in the markets of Monmurg than where we're at right now.

07:46.334 --> 07:46.734
[SPEAKER_00]: However,

07:48.058 --> 07:53.221
[SPEAKER_00]: I doubt anyone would be willing to try and run the blockade if there is one.

07:55.702 --> 08:00.925
[SPEAKER_01]: When you say run it, you mean closer to Monmurg if they actually have a cordon of ships?

08:01.825 --> 08:02.265
[SPEAKER_00]: Correct.

08:02.345 --> 08:04.126
[SPEAKER_00]: We have no idea what's out there.

08:04.266 --> 08:14.452
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that these two men-of-war brought us into Redshore and seem to have collected many of the traders that should have been heading towards Monmurg shows that

08:17.784 --> 08:24.626
[SPEAKER_00]: If we were intercepted on the seas, the Duke of Gradsul would have no problem taking all of our wares from us.

08:25.646 --> 08:28.127
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a risk we're not willing to take.

08:28.567 --> 08:39.731
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, and I would never want to set the seas more myself and the rest of the passengers to be in prison for erasmus.

08:40.251 --> 08:47.133
[SPEAKER_01]: If we are incapable to go around Jetson, Jetson Island,

08:50.061 --> 08:54.104
[SPEAKER_01]: So Steve, your audio is very, very quiet.

08:54.184 --> 08:58.007
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you close to the speaker or what's going on?

09:06.725 --> 09:09.187
[SPEAKER_01]: Um, I've actually got my headset on.

09:09.227 --> 09:10.568
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me if it's...

09:10.668 --> 09:11.548
[SPEAKER_00]: It's getting better now.

09:11.608 --> 09:15.471
[SPEAKER_00]: If you put the thing close to your mouth, I think he'll hear better.

09:15.491 --> 09:16.392
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

09:16.552 --> 09:19.554
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that better?

09:19.594 --> 09:21.315
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's actually much better.

09:21.815 --> 09:22.976
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, good.

09:23.256 --> 09:24.617
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so the guy says...

09:29.921 --> 09:31.522
[SPEAKER_00]: These are all risky things.

09:32.803 --> 09:33.743
[SPEAKER_01]: Very much, very much.

09:34.164 --> 09:34.624
[SPEAKER_00]: I, um...

09:36.940 --> 09:37.260
[SPEAKER_01]: Hmm.

09:38.121 --> 09:42.644
[SPEAKER_01]: Before you think further, I pour him my Philips glass a little bit more.

09:42.664 --> 09:50.270
[SPEAKER_01]: But I also make like a raised eyebrow, but like not to invite you to excess with drink.

09:50.811 --> 10:04.562
[SPEAKER_01]: But if this cordon, if this embargo of Monmurg is as effective as what we would see by all of these ships being moored, I would imagine

10:05.431 --> 10:15.656
[SPEAKER_01]: That the merchants stand to make perhaps three times as much as they would have by successfully making it into Monmurg.

10:17.457 --> 10:18.558
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a good point.

10:20.479 --> 10:22.520
[SPEAKER_00]: War does bring good coin.

10:25.361 --> 10:27.202
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the linchpin good captain.

10:27.282 --> 10:32.745
[SPEAKER_01]: Both your skill on the sea, your knowledge, and your willingness to risk.

10:34.186 --> 10:55.434
[SPEAKER_01]: What I can say is that my purpose is the purest in the sense that I just seek to entreat with Monmurg the princes who have great money and funds to assist a deed and help preserve their participation in the Iron Age.

10:56.815 --> 10:57.415
[SPEAKER_00]: I understand.

10:58.515 --> 11:02.597
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in truth, I cannot give you an answer right now.

11:04.078 --> 11:12.163
[SPEAKER_00]: I do desire what you desire for different reasons, but I do wish to make that journey to the markets in Monmurg.

11:12.984 --> 11:20.508
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm willing to wait some time, a few weeks in fact, to see if the passage will open up.

11:22.270 --> 11:24.991
[SPEAKER_01]: But, good sir, I've never heard of a war.

11:26.252 --> 11:28.754
[SPEAKER_01]: Hostilities being resolved within two weeks.

11:28.814 --> 11:30.455
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's Monmurg Sally's for?

11:31.471 --> 11:35.273
[SPEAKER_01]: And I have no understanding as to why they have not yet done that.

11:35.753 --> 11:38.234
[SPEAKER_00]: Well then, we need to get that understanding.

11:39.614 --> 11:40.735
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you understand me?

11:40.755 --> 11:43.676
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you say go around Jetsam Island.

11:45.877 --> 11:46.938
[SPEAKER_00]: You say go north.

11:47.418 --> 11:50.239
[SPEAKER_00]: We have no idea what the Monmurgian Navy is up to.

11:52.219 --> 12:05.249
[SPEAKER_00]: And I doubt I would be willing to risk my ship or my cargo just as other captains would be unwilling to do that themselves unless they knew more about the territory.

12:06.890 --> 12:07.691
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, of course.

12:08.051 --> 12:11.614
[SPEAKER_00]: So what I suggest is this, my good sir.

12:12.514 --> 12:15.477
[SPEAKER_00]: I speak to the captains among the other shipping vessels.

12:16.442 --> 12:21.364
[SPEAKER_00]: Which I intended to do in the first place, just to be able to facilitate trade.

12:22.244 --> 12:29.806
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps I could get some rumors, as it were, about what was happening right now.

12:31.527 --> 12:32.667
[SPEAKER_01]: I can go ashore.

12:32.927 --> 12:40.370
[SPEAKER_01]: I was thinking of asking the sergeant for at least an audience with maybe the master of the docks or the ports.

12:47.870 --> 12:56.712
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if that would bring any information, but I could do that and try to be back before sunrise.

12:59.993 --> 13:00.693
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be good.

13:01.733 --> 13:04.814
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps the local lord would also be somebody you could meet with.

13:05.554 --> 13:05.794
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

13:06.514 --> 13:08.014
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's what I was thinking.

13:08.034 --> 13:11.975
[SPEAKER_01]: At least somebody that's at least in court.

13:14.496 --> 13:14.696
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

13:15.457 --> 13:21.021
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one thing I do want to ask you again, you never answered me.

13:21.041 --> 13:25.144
[SPEAKER_01]: You do have some influence with Prince Jeon.

13:46.187 --> 13:46.487
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

13:47.888 --> 13:48.989
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, all right.

13:51.050 --> 13:57.275
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, know that I'm sure all these ships would greatly desire to make the travel to Monmurg.

13:59.977 --> 14:04.039
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know that we can need a flotilla to break the Black King.

14:04.580 --> 14:10.384
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, please, keep that at your closest to your heart.

14:10.524 --> 14:12.005
[SPEAKER_01]: Closer, even, than your heart.

14:13.538 --> 14:22.324
[SPEAKER_01]: If it gets out that I have any influence whatsoever against Jude, I'll be tortured to find out what that influence is, I'm sure.

14:23.145 --> 14:23.765
[SPEAKER_00]: I understand.

14:23.785 --> 14:27.008
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

14:27.288 --> 14:27.888
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

14:29.970 --> 14:30.991
[SPEAKER_00]: Let us talk again.

14:31.831 --> 14:35.294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I will return in the morning if I am able.

14:37.055 --> 14:41.298
[SPEAKER_00]: Alright, he nods, and do you want to go talk to the sergeant?

14:42.535 --> 14:49.696
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I consult really quickly with Forrest.

14:52.637 --> 15:01.499
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any member of the crew that you have played dice with or come to understand in any intimate way in our journey?

15:02.099 --> 15:03.139
[SPEAKER_01]: Forrest?

15:03.559 --> 15:05.600
[SPEAKER_00]: You mean, is there anyone I trust on board?

15:07.280 --> 15:09.060
[SPEAKER_00]: Most of these boys are trustworthy.

15:09.501 --> 15:10.221
[SPEAKER_00]: What would you ish?

15:12.674 --> 15:19.756
[SPEAKER_01]: You and I will go ashore, if the sergeant can at least point us in the right direction to speak.

15:20.256 --> 15:21.656
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll be ashore through the night.

15:21.957 --> 15:23.217
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd like to be able to return.

15:23.697 --> 15:30.559
[SPEAKER_01]: However, should it be dangerous to return?

15:31.239 --> 15:38.221
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe a lit, you know, lantern?

15:39.061 --> 15:40.102
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll talk to the bosun.

15:40.702 --> 15:41.422
[SPEAKER_00]: I know what you mean.

15:42.033 --> 15:45.794
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I would rather not come back to the ship if it's not a good idea.

15:46.214 --> 15:47.775
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I'll have a war with the bosun.

15:48.975 --> 15:50.376
[SPEAKER_01]: And please, slip him some coin.

15:50.436 --> 15:51.676
[SPEAKER_01]: I gave him, like, five gold.

15:52.056 --> 15:52.417
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

15:53.917 --> 15:54.957
[SPEAKER_00]: So he walks off.

15:55.678 --> 15:56.738
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

15:57.058 --> 15:59.859
[SPEAKER_01]: I tell him to be ready to go, though, and possibly not return.

16:00.399 --> 16:08.322
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'll go talk to the sergeant now, but I want to make sure that Forrest will be attired appropriately.

16:10.250 --> 16:13.672
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so you basically want to take all your stuff.

16:14.392 --> 16:22.876
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I mean, I don't need to take extra clothes or luggage, but the important crap, yeah.

16:23.156 --> 16:26.717
[SPEAKER_00]: Your spell book, all that stuff, right?

16:26.737 --> 16:28.658
[SPEAKER_01]: All that stuff.

16:29.439 --> 16:36.222
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the stuff that in case I'm not getting back onto the ship that I would definitely need to continue forward.

16:36.742 --> 16:38.443
[SPEAKER_00]: All your gold, all of that.

16:39.840 --> 16:40.780
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, one last thing.

16:40.800 --> 16:42.221
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, absolutely.

16:42.301 --> 16:45.422
[SPEAKER_00]: Not your case of a DN wine, but... Oh, no.

16:45.682 --> 16:47.762
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to lug around, like, heavy crap.

16:49.583 --> 16:55.905
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'll leave, or if I can stash a certain portion of gold in the room, and maybe take half of my gold.

16:56.465 --> 16:56.905
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

16:57.145 --> 16:59.006
[SPEAKER_00]: So you have a thousand gold coins with you.

16:59.026 --> 17:00.826
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to take fifty... five hundred gold.

17:01.326 --> 17:02.667
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'll take about five hundred gold.

17:02.727 --> 17:03.127
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that...

17:03.307 --> 17:04.027
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, is that like...

17:04.107 --> 17:06.628
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, like, how much is five hundred gold coins each coin?

17:07.498 --> 17:09.259
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's not that much.

17:09.919 --> 17:11.060
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

17:11.140 --> 17:12.381
[SPEAKER_01]: If I got a bag of holding, great.

17:12.541 --> 17:13.321
[SPEAKER_01]: If I don't...

17:13.361 --> 17:14.382
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like five pounds.

17:15.162 --> 17:15.842
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

17:16.523 --> 17:18.264
[SPEAKER_01]: Alright.

17:19.384 --> 17:23.666
[SPEAKER_01]: Did I ever get... Did Karmirg leave the battle pennant?

17:24.027 --> 17:26.248
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only reason why is I'm just speaking to the DM.

17:27.669 --> 17:33.912
[SPEAKER_01]: I would be mildly interested, super curious, if we ran Karmirg's pennant up the line,

17:35.012 --> 17:46.282
[SPEAKER_01]: By dawn, I know it's dark now, but if at dawn, just on the off chance that Gouge's reaver ship is disguised as a merchant vessel and is in this hangarage.

17:46.322 --> 17:47.263
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, exactly.

17:47.803 --> 17:53.288
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm totally thinking about running Karmirg's battle pennant.

17:53.388 --> 17:59.494
[SPEAKER_01]: And I did say that I wanted to get it if you left it.

17:59.534 --> 18:00.355
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you could do that.

18:03.252 --> 18:04.013
[SPEAKER_00]: Flaid skin.

18:04.073 --> 18:05.033
[SPEAKER_00]: Flaid giant skin.

18:07.334 --> 18:09.175
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the face of a giant.

18:09.215 --> 18:10.895
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the flesh of a giant's face.

18:11.155 --> 18:13.156
[SPEAKER_00]: Or something like that.

18:13.196 --> 18:16.137
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it'd be like Game of Thrones, the Flaid Man.

18:16.558 --> 18:19.719
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

18:20.139 --> 18:27.122
[SPEAKER_01]: Totally garish, but just on the odd chance that Gouge and Karmirg are sitting in Anchorage, you know, eating.

18:28.120 --> 18:29.741
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so that's my idea.

18:29.982 --> 18:36.646
[SPEAKER_01]: As a last-minute thing, I ask Horace to have the bosun run that up before dawn, before first light.

18:54.631 --> 19:02.513
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe without asking the captain, but just, you know, give the bosun an extra gold coin just to do that, to run it up before first light.

19:03.593 --> 19:07.834
[SPEAKER_01]: And he can take it down by mid-morning or if the captain asks, what the hell is that?

19:08.654 --> 19:11.575
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, well, you know, you got paid to run it up.

19:12.015 --> 19:13.955
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

19:14.015 --> 19:22.197
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like in place of, like, I don't want it, like, you know, to take the place of, like, the top pennant, you know, for the ship, but run it up, like, right below it.

19:24.598 --> 19:25.479
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what I mean?

19:25.499 --> 19:29.761
[SPEAKER_01]: Like how we have national flags, stars and bars, and then we've got state flags underneath it?

19:30.241 --> 19:32.342
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I don't think it seems like it's a problem.

19:32.883 --> 19:33.323
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

19:35.064 --> 19:38.726
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just as a... just a shot in the dark.

19:39.446 --> 19:40.607
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

19:42.008 --> 19:42.888
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll go talk to the sergeant.

19:45.049 --> 19:45.410
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

19:45.550 --> 19:51.153
[SPEAKER_00]: So you go to the sergeant, and basically he says, You wish to disembark?

19:52.678 --> 20:01.201
